// A tagged enum's payload reaches the refcount too — and this fixture exists as much for the DIAGNOSTIC
// as for the rejection.
//
// The rejection already worked (the verifier walks variant payloads as it walks fields). What did not work,
// once, was naming the culprit: the message was built by searching the type's FIELDS, and an enum has
// none, so it fell through to a generic phrase spliced after a possessive — rendering, verbatim:
//
//     cannot send `E` over a channel — its a field shares a non-atomic refcount across isolates
//
// for the one shape whose culprit is easiest to name precisely. It now says which variant and which
// payload. A diagnostic that cannot name what it is complaining about is how a reader concludes the
// compiler is confused rather than that their type is.
import { std::concurrent::Channel, std::concurrent::Sender, std::concurrent::Receiver, std::memory::Shared };

type value Leaf { int32 v; }

type enum E implements Sendable { A, B(Shared<Leaf> s) }

fn int32 main() {
    Channel<E> ch = Channel.bounded(capacity: 2);   // ERROR: variant `B`'s payload `s` holds the refcount
    return 0;
}
